# §5's private preference doc, which holds real salary figures.
#
# Excluded because Next.js **output file tracing** copies it into
# `.next/standalone/` all by itself: `preferences.ts` calls
# `fs.readFileSync(PREFERENCES_FILE)`, the tracer resolves that path statically,
# and the file rides along into the standalone bundle that the Dockerfile's
# runner stage copies wholesale. The Dockerfile's own COPY lines never name
# `private/`, which is why the leak was invisible to reading them — and why §23
# recorded, wrongly, that the deployed container has no preference doc.
#
# Keeping it out of the build context is what actually stops it. Nothing breaks:
# `loadPreferences()` already tolerates the file being absent, returns defaults,
# and sets `present: false`, which the admin route surfaces on the card.
private

# Review artifacts written at runtime into the bind-mounted data/ volume.
# Excluded from the build context so a pending proposal sitting on the host at
# build time is not baked into an image layer — the mount supplies the real one.
data/proposals
